 \section{What the results show and what they do not}\label{sec:discussion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} Against the identity-replacement attacker, targeting new identities lowers the gain that free replacement buys: R3 leaves a lower gain than uniform auditing on every sampled market, with its efficiency condition met. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood, and the advantage is not specific to R3: a rule that ranks identities by verified history does about as well, and in the factorial either the priority phase or newest-first targeting alone recovers most of the effect. The finding is therefore about age- or history-ranked scrutiny of new identities as a class, not about R3's particular design. Pacing contributes little at the studied load, and the terminal reserve that pacing adds is the source of R3's collapse at high load, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
+\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} Against the identity-replacement attacker, targeting new identities lowers the gain that free replacement buys: R3 leaves a lower gain than uniform auditing on every sampled market, with its efficiency condition met. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood, and the advantage is not specific to R3: a rule that ranks identities by verified history does about as well, and in the factorial either the priority phase or newest-first targeting alone recovers most of the effect. Within the scope of Section~\ref{sec:design}, the finding therefore holds across the tested age-ranked variants and the selected verified-history comparator; it is not specific to R3's particular design. Pacing contributes little at the studied load, and the terminal reserve that pacing adds is the source of R3's collapse at high load, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
 
 \paragraph{What they do not support.} The welfare attack without a profit constraint lowers the efficiency of every core rule by a similar amount, so the targeting rules do not protect welfare against an attacker who will accept losses. Robustness against the strongest searched family is unanswered wherever the doubling check flagged the search, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit, and we did not verify that the decisions are unchanged under the registered ones.
 % END-EV2
@@ -277,7 +277,7 @@ All values below come from the report that the tagged code wrote; the addendum b
 
 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-Free identity replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits, and directing audits at new or unverified identities restores much of its force. Two registered tests, the second on fresh markets drawn after the lock, show that this holds in sign across the sampled markets but not with the margin first claimed, that a verified-history rule matches the age-ranked rule we proposed, and that neither protects efficiency against loss-accepting welfare attacks. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is public.
+Free identity replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits, and in our tests directing audits at new or unverified identities reduces the measured replacement gain. Two registered tests, the second on fresh markets drawn after the lock, show that this holds in sign across the sampled markets but not with the margin first claimed, that a verified-history rule matches the age-ranked rule we proposed, and that neither protects efficiency against loss-accepting welfare attacks. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is public.
 % END-EV2
 
 \paragraph{Availability.} The public release at the report commit contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the paper sources, and a one-command reproduction of every table.

